Dijkstra's Algorithm for Routing Table Design
15-Minute Presentation Speech Script

Slide 1 - Title (about 1 minute)
Good morning/afternoon everyone.
Today I will present Dijkstra's Algorithm for Routing Table Design, focusing on how shortest-path algorithms are used in real networks to find least-cost routes.

In modern communication systems, routing is one of the most critical tasks. Routers must continuously decide how to forward packets efficiently, even when the network has many nodes and links. If routing decisions are poor, we get higher delay, congestion, and packet loss. If routing decisions are optimized, we get faster and more reliable communication.

This is where Dijkstra's algorithm becomes important.
It provides a systematic and mathematically correct way to compute shortest paths from one source node to all other nodes in a graph. Because of this, it is a core building block of link-state routing protocols such as OSPF and IS-IS, which are widely used in enterprise and ISP networks.

In this presentation, I will explain the problem, key concepts, algorithm steps, a worked example, and finally how shortest paths are converted into routing table entries.


